main.content:
==English==
{{wp|[[go (verb)|go]]}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|gō}}
* {{a|en|UK}}
** {{IPA|en|/ɡəʊ/|a=RP}}
*** {{audio|en|en-uk-to go.ogg|text=to go|a=RP}}
*** {{IPA|en|/ɡə/|a=weak form}}
** {{IPA|en|/ɡu/ [ɡʊw]|a={{w|West Midlands}}}}
** {{IPA|en|/ɡæn/|a=Northumbria}}
** {{IPA|en|/ɡo/|a=Scotland}}
** {{IPA|en|/ɡoː/|a=Wales,non-toe-tow}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɡoʊ/|a=GA}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-go.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|[ɡoː]|a=CA}}
* {{IPA|en|[ɡəʉ]|a=Australia}}
** {{audio|en|EN-AU ck1 go.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{IPA|en|[ɡɐʉ]|a=New Zealand}}
* {{hyph|en|go}}
* {{homophone|en|geaux<aa:toe-tow>}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊ|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:gon|id=move|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|gon}}, {{m|enm|goon}}, from {{inh|en|ang|gān|t=to go}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*gān}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*gāną|t=to go}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ǵʰeh₁-|t=to leave}}.

The inherited past tense form {{m|en|yode}} (compare {{cog|ang|ēode}}) was replaced through [[suppletion]] in the 15th century by {{m|en|went}}, from {{cog|ang|wendan|t=to go, depart, wend}}.
{{col-top|2|cognates and related terms}}
Cognate with {{cog|sco|gae}}, {{m|sco|gan}}, {{m|sco|gang}}, {{m|sco|ging}}, {{m|sco|gyang|t=to go}}, {{cog|yol|go}}, {{m|yol|goe}}, {{m|yol|goeth}}, {{m|yol|gow|t=to go}}, {{cog|fy|gean|t=to go}}, {{cog|gsw|gaa}}, {{m|gsw|go|t=to go, walk, step}}, {{cog|bar|geh|t=to go}}, {{cog|cim|ghéenan}}, {{m|cim|gian|t=to go}}, {{cog|nl|gaan|t=to go}}, {{cog|nds-nl|gan}}, {{m|nds-nl|gaon|t=to go}}, {{cog|de|gehen|t=to go}}, {{cog|nds-de|gahn|t=to go}}, {{cog|li|gaon}}, {{m|li|goëne|t=to go}}, {{cog|lb|goen|t=to go}}, {{cog|wym|gejn}}, {{m|wym|gyjn|t=to go}}, {{cog|yi|גיין|t=to go, walk}}, {{cog|da,nb,nn,sv|gå|t=to go, walk}}, {{cog|gme-cgo|geen|t=to go}}, {{cog|got|𐌲𐌰𐌲𐌲𐌰𐌽|t=to go}}. Compare also {{cog|sq|ngah|t=to run, drive, go}}, {{cog|grc|κιχάνω|t=to meet with, arrive at}}, {{cog|ae|𐬰𐬀𐬰𐬁𐬨𐬌}}, {{cog|sa|जहाति|tr=jáhāti|t=to shun, avoid, lay aside}}.
{{col-bottom}}

====Alternative forms====
* {{alt|en|gan||Northumbrian}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|go<goes,,went:yode[obsolete]:goed[nonstandard],gone:went[nonstandard]>}}

# To [[move]], either physically or in an abstract sense:
#: {{syn|en|move|fare|tread|draw|drift|wend|cross}}
#: {{ant|en|freeze|halt|remain|stand still|stay|stop}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[move]] through [[space]] (especially to or through a place). {{q|May be used of tangible things such as people or cars, or intangible things such as moods or information.}}
##: {{ux|en|Why don’t you '''go''' with us?}}
##: {{ux|en|This train '''goes''' through Cincinnati on its way to Chicago.}}
##: {{ux|en|Chris, where are you '''going'''?}}
##: {{ux|en|There's no public transit where I'm '''going'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|Wow, look at him '''go'''!}}
##: {{ux|en|The rumour '''went''' all around town.}}
##* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=VI|passage=She was so mad she wouldn't speak to me for quite a spell, but at last I coaxed her into '''going''' up to Miss Emmeline's room and fetching down a tintype of the missing Deacon man.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=David Neilson|title=Standstill|isbn=1412055954|page=159|passage={{...}} there was a general sense of panic '''going''' through the house; {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Mike Vouri|title=The Pig War: Standoff at Griffin Bay|isbn=0914019627|page=177|passage=Telegrams to London '''went''' by wire to Halifax, Nova Scotia, thence by steam mail packet to Liverpool, {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[move]] or [[travel]] through [[time]] (either literally—in a fictional or hypothetical situation in which [[time travel]] is possible—or in one's mind or knowledge of the historical record). {{q|See also ''[[go back]]''.}}
##: {{ux|en|Yesterday was the second-wettest day on record; you have to '''go''' all the way back to 1896 to find a day when more rain fell.}}
##: {{ux|en|Fans want to see the Twelfth Doctor '''go''' to the 51st century to visit River in the library.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=September 18 2002|journal=Congressional Record: Proceedings and Debates of the 107th Congress, second session; Senate|page=17033
|passage=You have to '''go''' all the way back to Herbert Hoover to see a performance in the Standard & Poors 500 equal to what we are experiencing right now.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Charlotte Sadler|title=Time for One More Dance|isbn=1452015325|page=162|passage="I don't know how to tell you this, Aubrey, but you can't go back to 1938 {{...}} the program won't accept any date that I input before 1941." {{...}} "Well, I'll '''go''' to 1941, then."}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[navigate]] (to a file or folder on a computer, a site on the internet, a memory, etc.).
##: {{ux|en|For the best definitions, '''go''' to wiktionary.org}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=David J. Clark|title=The Unofficial Guide to Microsoft Office Word 2007|isbn=0470377437|page=536
|passage=To access Office-related TechNet resources, '''go''' to www.microsoft.com/technet/prodtechnol/office.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Lisa W. Coyne; Amy R. Murrell|title=The Joy of Parenting|isbn=157224593X
|passage='''Go''' to your earliest memory and to your favorite one, then to one that's difficult to consider.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Glen E. Clarke; Edward Tetz|title=CompTIA A+ Certification All-in-One For Dummies|isbn=1118223691|page=280
|passage='''Go''' to drive C: through My Computer (or Computer in Windows 7 and Vista) and double-click the c:\data folder.}}
## To [[move]] (a particular distance, or in a particular fashion).
##: {{ux|en|We've only '''gone''' twenty miles today.}}
##: {{ux|en|This car can '''go''' circles around that one.}}
##: {{ux|en|The fight '''went''' the distance and was decided on points.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=Harrison E. Salisbury|title=The 900 Days: The Siege of Leningrad|isbn=0306812983|page=307
|passage=The car '''went''' a short distance, then halted. There was something wrong with the carburetor.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[move]] or [[travel]] in order to do something, or to do something while moving.
##: {{ux|en|We '''went''' swimming.}}
##: {{ux|en|Let's '''go''' shopping.}}
##: {{ux|en|Please '''go''' and get me some envelopes.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[leave]]; to move away.
##: {{syn|en|depart|leave|exit|go away|go out}}
##: {{ant|en|come|arrive|approach}}
##: {{ux|en|Please don't '''go'''!}}
##: {{ux|en|I really must be '''going'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|Workmen were coming and '''going''' at all hours of the night.}}
##* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|year=1596.2|book=IV|canto=XI|stanza=39|page=167|passage=And following Dee, which Britons long '''ygone''' / Did call diuine, that doth by Cheſter tend; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|XII||Like her I '''go'''; I cannot stay;<br>⁠I leave this mortal ark behind,<br>A weight of nerves without a mind,<br>And leave the cliffs, and haste away […]}}
## To [[follow]] or proceed according to (a course or path).
##: {{ux|en|Let's '''go''' this way for a while.}}
##: {{ux|en|She was '''going''' that way anyway, so she offered to show him where it was.}}
## To [[travel]] or pass along.
##: {{ux|en|We '''went''' the full length of the promenade before we found a place to sit down.}}
##: {{ux|en|His life story '''goes''' the gamut, from poverty-stricken upbringing to colossal wealth.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete|intransitive}} To [[walk]]; to travel on one's feet. {{defdate|11th|19th c.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1485|author=w:Thomas Malory|title=w:Le Morte d'Arthur|section=Book XII
|passage=‘As for that,’ seyde Sir Trystram, ‘I may chose othir to ryde othir to '''go'''.’}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1624|author=John Smith|title=Generall Historie|publisher=Kupperman|year_published=1988|page=129
|passage=Master Piercie our new President, was so sicke hee could neither '''goe''' nor stand.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|chiefly|of a|machine}} To [[work]] or [[function]] (properly); to move or perform (as required).
#: {{syn|en|function|work|operate|run}}
#: {{ux|en|The engine just won't '''go''' anymore.}}
#: {{ux|en|Don't put your hand inside while the motor's '''going'''!}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1997|journal=New Scientist|volume=154|page=105
|passage='Although the lemon is now black and shrivelled the motor is still '''going''' strong. If I can make my small motor run for month after month on a single lemon, just imagine how much "juice" there must be in a whole sackful', Mr Ashill said.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Michael Buckley|title=Shangri-La: A Practical Guide to the Himalayan Dream|isbn=1841622044|page=146|passage={{...}} though his publisher swears black and blue that Kelder is still '''going''' strong and still remains an intensely private person.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[start]]; to begin (an action or process).
#: {{ux|en|You've got thirty seconds to solve the anagram, starting now. '''Go'''!}}
#* {{RQ:Congreve Old Batchelour|page=45|passage=At leaſt, I'm ſure I can fiſh it out of her. She's the very Sluce to her Lady's Secrets;—'Tis but ſetting her Mill '''agoing''', and I can drein her of 'em all.}}
#* '''2001''' June 18, a prophecy, quoted in ''Mary and the Unity of the Church'' {{ISBN|192658211X}}, page 49:
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To take a [[turn]], especially in a [[game]].
#: {{syn|en|move|make one's [[move]]|take one’s [[turn]]}}
#: {{ux|en|It’s your turn; '''go'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|I've got all vowels. I don't think I can '''go'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[attend]].
#: {{ux|en|I '''go''' to school at the schoolhouse.}}
#: {{ux|en|She '''went''' to Yale.}}
#: {{ux|en|They only '''go''' to church on Christmas.}}
# To [[proceed]]:
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[proceed]] (often in a specified manner, indicating the perceived quality of an event or state).
##: {{ux|en|That '''went''' well.}}
##: {{ux|en|"How are things '''going'''?" "Not bad, thanks."}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|II|i|passage=How '''goes''' the night, boy?}}
##* {{RQ:Arbuthnot Coins|passage=I think, as the world '''goes''', he was a good sort of man enough.}}
##* {{RQ:Watts Logick|passage=Whether the cause '''goes''' for me or against me, you must pay me the reward.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1986|journal=The Opera Quarterly|volume=4|issue=3-4|page=24
|passage=I certainly won't mention it to Ben, and will '''go''' carefully if he mentions it to me.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|colloquial|with another verb, sometimes linked by ''and''}} To [[proceed]] (especially to do something [[foolish]]).
##: {{ux|en|Why'd you have to '''go''' and do that?}}
##: {{ux|en|Why'd you have to '''go''' do that?}}
##: {{ux|en|He just '''went''' and punched the guy.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Debra Glass|title=Scarlet Widow|isbn=1419937901|page=96
|passage=And even if she had believed the story about a John Smith, she might '''go''' telling everyone in town about what she'd seen.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[extend]] along.
#: {{ux|en|The fence '''goes''' the length of the boundary.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[extend]] (from one point in time or space to another).
#: {{ux|en|This property '''goes''' all the way to the state line.}}
#: {{ux|en|The working week '''goes''' from Monday to Friday.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1946|title=Hearings Before the Joint Committee on the Investigation of the Pearl Harbor Attack, Congress of the United States, Seventy-ninth Congress, First Session|page=2459
|passage=I think those figures start from 1932 and '''go''' to 1941, inclusive, {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[lead]] (to a place); to give access (to).
#: {{ux|en|Does this road '''go''' to Fort Smith?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|title=Without Delusion|page=191|isbn=148369822X
|passage=“Where does this door '''go'''?” Bev asked as she pointed to a door painted a darker green than the powder green color of the carpet. Janet answered. “That door '''goes''' to the back yard.”}}
# To [[become]], [[move]] to or [[come]] to (a state, position, situation)
## {{senseid|en|become}} {{lb|en|copulative|rather|informal|followed by an adjective}} To {{l|en|become|id=copulative}} {{q|often used with colors and negative states}}.
##: {{par|en|become<id:copulative>|get<id:become>|turn<id:become>|come<id:become>|fall<id:become>|grow<id:become>|wax<id:become>}}
##: {{ux|en|You'll '''go''' blind.}}
##: {{ux|en|The milk '''went''' bad.}}
##: {{ux|en|I '''went''' crazy.}}
##: {{ux|en|After failing as a criminal, he decided to '''go''' straight.}}
##: {{ux|en|The video clip '''went''' viral.}}
##: {{ux|en|Don't tell my Mum: she'll '''go''' ballistic.}}
##: {{ux|en|The local shop wants to '''go''' digital, and eventually '''go''' global.}}
##: {{ux|en|The traffic light '''went''' straight from green to red.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Saverio Giovacchini|title=Hollywood Modernism: Film and Politics|isbn=1566398630|page=18
|passage=Referring to the American radicals who '''went''' Hollywood in the 1930s, Abraham Polonsky argues that "you can't possibly explain the Hollywood communists away {{...}}"}}
## To move to (a position or state).
##: {{ux|en|If we can win on Saturday, we'll '''go''' top of the league.}}
##: {{ux|en|They '''went''' level with their rivals.}}
## To come (to a certain condition or state).
##: {{ux|en|They '''went''' into debt.}}
##: {{ux|en|She '''goes''' to sleep around 10 o'clock.}}
# To [[assume]] the [[obligation]] or [[function]] of; to be, to serve as.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1912|title=The Bookseller, Newsdealer and Stationer|volume=36|page=17
|passage=There is scarcely a business man who is not occasionally asked to '''go''' bail for somebody.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|copulative}} To [[continuous]]ly or [[habitual]]ly be in a state.
#: {{ux|en|I don't want my children to '''go''' hungry.}}
#: {{ux|en|We '''went''' barefoot in the summer.}}
# To [[turn out]], to [[result]]; to come to (a certain result).
#: {{ux|en|The decision '''went''' the way we expected.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[tend]] (toward a result).
#: {{ux|en|Well, that '''goes''' to show you.}}
#: {{ux|en|These experiences '''go''' to make us stronger.}}
# To contribute to a (specified) end product or result.
#: {{ux|en|qualities that '''go''' to make a lady / lip-reader / sharpshooter}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1839|title=A Challenge to Phrenologists; Or, Phrenology Tested|page=155
|passage=What can we know of any substance or existence, but as made up of all the qualities that '''go''' to its composition: extension, solidity, form, colour; take these away, and you know nothing.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1907|author=Patrick Doyle|title=Indian Engineering|volume=41|page=181
|passage=The avoirdupois pound is one of 7,000 grains, and '''go''' to the pound.}}
# To [[pass]], to be used up:
## {{lb|en|intransitive|of time}} To [[elapse]], to [[pass]]; to slip away. {{q|Compare ''[[go by]]''.}}
##: {{ux|en|The time '''went''' slowly.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1850|title=Sketches of New England Character|magazine=Holden's Dollar Magazine|volume=5-6|page=731
|passage=But the days '''went''' and '''went''', and she never came; and then I thought I would come here where you were.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Sue Raymond|title=Hidden Secrets|isbn=1435747070|page=357
|passage=The rest of the morning '''went''' quickly and before Su knew it Jean was knocking on the door {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[end]] or [[disappear]]. {{q|Compare ''[[go away]]''.}}
##: {{syn|en|disappear|vanish|go away|end|dissipate}}
##: {{ant|en|remain|stay|hold}}
##: {{ux|en|After three days, my headache finally '''went'''.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[spend|spent]] or [[use up|used up]].
##: {{ux|en|His money '''went''' on drink.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Ross Macdonald|title=Black Money|isbn=0307759563|page=29|passage=All I have is a sleeping bag right now. All my money '''goes''' to keep up the cars.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[die]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:die}}
#: {{ux|en|I want to '''go''' in my sleep.}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Marmion|passage=By Saint George, he's '''gone'''! / That spear wound hath our master sped.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1978|author={{w|David Galloway (writer)|David Galloway}}|title=A Family Album|location=London|publisher={{w|Calder Publishing|John Calder}}|isbn=0714536822|page=36|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/familyalbumnovel0000gall/page/36/mode/2up?q=go|passage=After two years of swaddled invalidism, Mrs. Morton emitted a final gassy sigh and died, whereas twenty years later Elihu was to '''go''' “just like that,” as the neighbors said, from a stroke.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|cricket}} To be [[lose|lost]] or [[out]]:
## {{lb|en|intransitive|cricket|of a wicket}} To be [[lose|lost]].
##: {{ux|en|The third wicket '''went''' just before lunch.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|cricket|of a batsman}} To be [[out]].
##: {{ux|en|Smith bowls ... Jones hits it straight up in the air ... and ... caught! Jones has '''gone'''!}}
# To break down or apart:
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[collapse]] or give way, to break apart.
##: {{syn|en|crumble|collapse|disintegrate|give way}}
##: {{ux|en|Careful! It looks as if that ceiling could '''go''' at any moment!}}
##* {{RQ:Bramah Max Carrados Mysteries|passage='Back!' cried Carrados. 'The cliff is '''going'''!'<br>The cliff, as Mr Lidmarsh had remarked, had been '''going''' for centuries—going by inches, by feet, or by yards. {{...}} Now the cliff face—all the ground beyond the fissure—began to fall rigidly away {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Shaunti Feldhahn|title=The Lights of Tenth Street|isbn=0307564444
|passage=Sober-eyed commentators safe in their television studios interviewed engineers about the chances that the rest of the dam could '''go'''.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[break down]] or [[decay]].
##: {{ux|en|My mind is '''going'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|She's 83; her eyesight is starting to '''go'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[sell|sold]].
#: {{ux|en|The car '''went''' for five thousand dollars.}}
#: {{ux|en|The store is closing down so everything must '''go'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[discard]]ed or [[dispose]]d of.
#: {{ux|en|This chair has got to '''go'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|All this old rubbish can '''go'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[give]]n, especially to be [[assigned]] or [[allot]]ted.
#: {{ux|en|The property shall '''go''' to my wife.}}
#: {{ux|en|The award '''went''' to Steven Spielberg.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=David Bouchier|title=The Song of Suburbia: Scenes from Suburban Life|isbn=0595437575|page=19|passage=If my money '''goes''' to education, I want a report card.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To [[survive]] or [[get by]]; to last or persist for a stated length of time.
#: {{ux|en|How long can you '''go''' without water?}}
#: {{ux|en|We've '''gone''' without your help for a while now.}}
#: {{ux|en|I've '''gone''' ten days now without a cigarette.}}
#: {{ux|en|Can you two '''go''' twenty minutes without arguing?!}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|sports}} To have a certain [[record]].
#: {{ux|en|They've '''gone''' one for three in this series.}}
#: {{ux|en|The team is '''going''' five in a row.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1983|journal=Princeton Alumni Weekly|volume=84|page=48
|passage=Against the Big Green, Princeton '''went''' the entire first and third quarters without gaining a first down, {{...}}}}
#* '''2011''', H. R. F. Keating, <!--added to show "go" in this sense without the word "without"-->''Zen there was Murder'' {{ISBN|1448202426}}:
# To be authoritative, accepted, or valid:
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of an opinion or instruction, to have (final) [[authority]]; to be authoritative.
##: {{ux|en|Whatever the boss says '''goes''', do you understand?}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[accept]]ed.
##: {{ux|en|Anything '''goes''' around here.}}
##* {{RQ:Locke Value|passage=The money which remains should '''go''' according to its true value.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[valid]] or [[applicable]].
##: {{ux|en|The baked beans can go on this shelf, and the same '''goes''' for all these other tins.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Shayna Lance King|title=If You'd Read This Book: You'd Be Employed By Now|isbn=0692206221|page=22|passage=[To job interviews, wear] muted colors. No pink or paisley (that '''goes''' for you too, guys!) {{...}}}}
# {{senseid|en|makeasound}} To [[say]] (something), to make a sound:
## {{lb|en|transitive|colloquial}} To [[say]] (something, aloud or to oneself).
##: {{ux|en|I '''go''', "As if!" And she was all like, "Whatever!"}}
##: {{ux|en|As soon as I did it, I '''went''' "that was stupid."}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[make]] the (specified) sound.
##: {{ux|en|Cats '''go''' "meow". Motorcycles '''go''' "vroom".}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[sound]]; to make a noise.
##: {{ux|en|I woke up just before the clock '''went'''.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=June 24 1992|author=w:Edwina Currie|journal=Diary
|passage=At 4pm, the phone '''went'''. It was {{w|The Sun}}: 'We hear your daughter's been expelled for cheating at her school exams{{...}}' / / She'd made a remark to a friend at the end of the German exam and had been [[pull up|pulled up]] for talking. / / As they left the exam room, she muttered that the teacher was a '[[twat]]'. He heard and [[flip]]ped{{mdash}}a pretty stupid thing to do, knowing the kids were tired and tense after exams. Instead of dropping it, the teacher complained to the [[head|Head]] and Deb was [[carpet]]ed.}}
# To be [[express]]ed or [[compose]]d (a certain way).
#: {{ux|en|The tune '''goes''' like this.}}
#: {{ux|en|As the story '''goes''', he got the idea for the song while sitting in traffic.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[resort]] (to).
#: {{ux|en|The nylon gears kept breaking, so we '''went''' to stainless steel.}}
# To [[apply]] or [[subject]] oneself to:
## To apply oneself; to undertake; to have as one's goal or intention. {{q|Compare ''[[be going to]]''.}}
##: {{ux|en|I'm '''going''' to join a sports team.}}
##: {{ux|en|I wish you'd '''go''' and get a job.}}
##: {{ux|en|He '''went''' to pick it up, but it rolled out of reach.}}
##: {{ux|en|He's '''going''' to leave town tomorrow.}}
##* {{RQ:Sidney Arcadia|passage=Seeing himself confronted by so many, like a resolute orator, he '''went''' not to denial, but to justify his cruel falsehood.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1990|author=Celestine Sibley|title=Tokens of myself|isbn=0929264401|page=73
|passage=Now I didn't '''go''' to make that mistake about the record-breaking drought of more than fifty years ago, but, boy, am I glad I made it. Otherwise, I wouldn't have heard from Joe Almand.}}
